  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nPer model consensus, the remnants of landfalling Hurricane Isaias \nalong the Gulf Coast will phase with a meandering upper low over the \nSouthern Plains the next few days, but widespread precipitation \nassociated with these features will remain well east of the forecast \narea. \n\nChances for rain/thunderstorms finally enter back into the forecast \nby Tuesday or Tuesday night and persist through Thursday or Thursday \nnight, as a cold front approaches from the northwest and a deep \nupper trough approaches from the west. Increasing shear coupled with \nmodest instability and strong forcing may support a handful of \nstrong to severe thunderstorms during this time period. \nAdditionally, well above average precipitable waters may support \nheavy rainfall. Stay tuned as we refine forecast details in the \ncoming days.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nAbove average heights/thickness will support continued above average \ntemperatures through about mid next week. Daytime temperatures a few \ndegrees either side of 90 degrees look probable, especially through \nMonday or Tuesday. We may be flirting with record highs Monday\nand Tuesday. Additionally, stout/gusty south to southwest winds\nshould gust upwards of 30-35 kts today through Monday, highest \ngenerally west of the KS Turnpike, owing to persistent low \npressure to the west- northwest of the region.\n\n\nAs we head into Thursday or Friday of next week, model consensus \nsupports a fairly drastic cool down in wake of the aforementioned \nstorm system, as stout/chilly Canadian high pressure settles \nsoutheast into Mid-America. Deterministic consensus supports daytime \nhighs in the 50s-60s and overnight lows in the 30s-40s by Friday and \nFriday night. For now, only went with 60s-70s for highs and 40s-50s \nfor lows.",
